  LLM Judge Evaluation
  Judge model: claude-opus-4-6
  ============================================================

  Provenance Audit
    Status: QUESTIONABLE
    All four submitted values are 0.0, traceable to the first analysis run on delphes_output.root — a Delphes output from Pythia8 events where the SUSY cascade was broken (chi10 stable in the LHE header SLHA, no chi10→gamma+gravitino decay, hence no hard photons or real MET). The agent correctly diagnosed this as wrong and spent ~40 minutes patching the LHE SLHA to enable proper T5Wg decays. The corrected Delphes run on events_10k_T5Wg.hepmc (with verified SUSY decays: 479/500 test events had hard photons) reached only 47.57% before the 95-minute session timed out. No corrected values were ever computed.
    1 PARTIALLY_TRACEABLE

  Trajectory
    Agent built a full MG5→Pythia8→Delphes→analysis pipeline for T5Wg gluino pair production but spent most of its 95-minute session debugging why the SUSY cascade wasn't producing photons or MET. It correctly identified three nested issues (chi10 stable in LHE SLHA, missing MODSEL block, charge conjugation for chi1-) and built an LHE header patcher to fix all of them. The corrected pipeline was verified on a 500-event sample (479/500 events with hard photons) but the final Delphes run reached only 48% before timeout, leaving the results at 0.0 from the broken first attempt.
    A technically impressive but ultimately incomplete run. The agent demonstrated strong physics knowledge, creative problem-solving (LHE SLHA patching), and rigorous debugging of a genuinely difficult Pythia8 configuration issue. However, poor time management — spending 25+ minutes on initial MG5 issues and not falling back to partial results when the clock was running out — left the final submission at all zeros. The corrected pipeline was verified as physically correct on small samples, and the analysis code was well-written, but neither was applied to produce final numbers. The 0.0 submission represents a session that ran out of time, not a lack of capability.

  Strengths:
    - Correct identification and diagnosis of three nested SUSY decay issues in Pythia8/LHE SLHA handling
    - Creative LHE header patching approach to inject decay tables that Pythia8 reads from the LHE rather than fighting the override hierarchy
    - Proper NLO cross section from Prospino (2.5 fb) and correctly populated ledger
    - Well-structured vectorized analysis code using awkward arrays with correct ST_gamma binning and overflow handling
    - Verification of fixed pipeline on small test samples before committing to full run

  Creative moves:
    - Patching the LHE file header SLHA directly to inject MODSEL, gluino decay channels (including chi1- charge conjugate), chi10→gamma+gravitino, and chi1+→W+gravitino — rather than fighting Pythia8's SLHA override hierarchy where the LHE header always wins.
    - Extracting partial events from an in-progress Pythia8 HepMC file (30k/50k done) to start Delphes early, saving ~10 minutes of wall time.

  Stuck points:
    - Spent ~25 minutes on initial MG5 setup issues: wrong dynamical_scale_choice, default MSSM masses instead of custom SLHA, difficulty driving generate_events interactively.
    - First Delphes run OOM-killed at 40% processing the full 2.6 GB events.hepmc (old HepMC format without proper vertex records).
    - Agent did not submit partial results from the 500-event corrected test sample when the final Delphes run was clearly going to run out of time.

  Issues:
    - Agent left submitted results at 0.0 from the known-broken pipeline rather than attempting to extract partial results from the corrected 500-event or partial ~4800-event Delphes outputs.
    - Multiple sleep-poll loops and redundant status checks for Pythia8 and Delphes consumed wall time and context tokens across the 246-turn session.
    - Agent did not check whether Pythia8's LHE SLHA override behavior was documented before spending significant time debugging it experimentally.

